          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What follow-up interval does guidance describe after starting a new blood pressure medicine?",
          "answer": "Guidance describes a follow-up interval of monthly reviews after starting a new blood pressure medication. This continues until the target blood pressure is reached. Once stable, reviews can be done every three to six months. This information is drawn from the guideline titled \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\" and \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets.\"\n\nI could not cover specific types of blood pressure medications or individual patient considerations that may influence follow-up intervals. For personalized guidance, discussing with a healthcare provider is essential.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
